      "SummaryText": "ric Acid: No changes have been made to the existing system.\n\nG. Worst-Case and Alternative Scenarios\n- Hydrofluoric Acid:\n  - Worst-case: Complete release of entire inventory as the result of  a violent storm (Tornado or Hurricane) that leveled the building and destroyed the tanks and smaller containers within the warehouse.  With the warehouse gone, there would no longer be a structure around the spill that would contain the liquid  or prevent it from being completely released into the environment resulting in full vaporization and formation of a toxic vapor cloud.  Such a catastrophic release would be extremely unlikely - but it is the worst case scenario for this chemical.  If this occurred, the  toxic end point would be approximately 16 miles and the affected population would be approximately 180,462 people.  As a footnote, if the entire inventory of approximately 2.7 million pounds was released, but contained within the building, the radius would be 3 miles and the affected population would be 6,345 people. \n  - Alternative Release Scenario: Line or hose rupture during repackaging, with the spill largely contained and cleaned before significant vaporization occurs.  The warehouse and the containment area in which the tanks are located would contain the spill - even if the contents of an entire tank were lost.  This release would result in a vapor cloud that would extend for approximately 0.1 miles and would affect a population of approximately 7 people.\n\n- Formaldehyde:\n  - Worst-case: Complete release of entire inventory as the result of  a violent storm (Tornado or Hurricane) that leveled the building and destroyed the tanks and smaller containers within the warehouse.  With the warehouse gone, there would no longer be a structure around the spill that would contain the liquid  or prevent it from being completely released into the environment resulting in full vaporization and formation of a toxic vapor cloud.  Such a catastrophic release would be extremely u"
