    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122031,
      "SummaryText": "Introduction and Chemical Overview\n\nIn the previous Risk Management Plan (RMP), both the catastrophic loss scenarios and more probable alternatives were evaluated using the EPA-provided RMP software.  Brainerd, LLC currently stores two chemicals onsite\u0097Formaldehyde and Hydrofluoric Acid\u0097in quantities exceeding the RMP threshold limits.\n\nA. Accidental Release Prevention Policies\nThe facility has implemented comprehensive accidental release prevention policies.\n- Hydrofluoric Acid: Daily inspections are conducted in the storage area. These inspections include checks of the scrubber, sump, tank levels, hoses, connections, and for any signs of leaks.\n- Formaldehyde: A checklist is used during each loading operation to verify tank levels and prevent overfilling, thereby minimizing the risk of a release.\n\nB. Emergency Response Policy\nThe facility\u0092s emergency response procedures are outlined in the Integrated Contingency Plan (ICP). This plan includes:\n- Emergency contact information\n- Materials and methods for containment\n- Notification protocols for the Fire Department and Local Emergency Planning Committee (LEPC) if onsite personnel cannot manage the incident.\n\nC. Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)\nThe facility maintains SOPs and a contingency plan that address both general and chemical-specific release prevention measures.\n- Employees receive annual training on these procedures, including specific protocols for handling Formaldehyde and Hydrofluoric Acid.\n\nD. Accident History\nThe facility has not experienced any reportable accidents involving these chemicals in the past five years.\n\nE. Emergency Response Expectations\nWhile the facility has trained personnel, it is anticipated that their role in an emergency would be limited. The local fire department and HAZMAT teams would be contacted for full-scale response efforts.\n\nF. Facility Modifications\n- Formaldehyde: Additional tanks have been installed to support repackaging operations, reducing handling risks.\n- Hydrofluo"
